/** \page checkpointing Exact application-state checkpointing and replay

MFEM checkpointing operates on arbitrary replayable application state. A
checkpoint is complete only when it contains everything required to continue
deterministic replay. A solution Vector can be one component of that state, but
is not its generic definition.

The initial built-in specialization supports fixed-step ForwardEulerSolver.
The state-centric core is synchronous, independent of MPI and physical time,
and can also be used by application-defined nonlinear, optimization,
continuation, or adaptation iterations.

\section checkpointing_state_model State model and exactness

StateId is the ordered logical position of a replayable state. Depending on the
application, it can denote a time step, Newton iteration, optimization
iteration, continuation step, or mesh-adaptation cycle. StepId remains an alias
for StateId for time-stepping source compatibility. Neither type stores or
implies physical time.

For states S_i and deterministic transitions F_i, exact replay requires

\f[
\operatorname{Restore}(S_i) + F_i + \cdots + F_{j-1} = S_j.
\f]

CheckpointStateAdapter::Capture() must therefore include every value that can
affect continuation: primary fields, mesh or discretization state, time and
step size when present, adaptive-controller or multistep history, nonlinear
globalization state, material properties, continuation parameters, RNG state,
and relevant application metadata. The generic core cannot determine which of
these values are required.

CheckpointStateAdapter owns the capture format, compatibility checks, and safe
restore order. It borrows application objects; those objects must outlive the
adapter and controller. For dependent MFEM objects, an application adapter may
need to restore in an order such as

\code{.unparsed}
Mesh -> FiniteElementSpace -> GridFunction
\endcode

Mesh, ParMesh, FiniteElementSpace, and GridFunction serialization are not
implemented by this initial interface.

StatePropagator::Advance() applies the same deterministic transitions used in
the original computation. Its precondition is that the application represents
the supplied `from` state; on success the application represents `to`. The
meaning of a transition is entirely application-defined.

\section checkpointing_architecture Architecture

\code{.unparsed}
                 Arbitrary MFEM application
                           |
                           v
                CheckpointStateAdapter
              capture / restore full state
                           |
                           v
                  CheckpointController
                   /              \
                  v                v
        CheckpointSchedule     StatePropagator
                   \              /
                    \            /
                     v          v
                     exact replay
                           |
                           v
              ExactCheckpointWindow
                           |
                           v
                CheckpointStorage
                  memory / files
\endcode

CheckpointController, ExactCheckpointWindow, CheckpointSchedule, and the
storage implementations operate only on StateId, CheckpointId, and opaque
Snapshot values. They do not interpret Vector, ODESolver,
TimeDependentOperator, or physical time.

The implemented ODE specialization is

\code{.unparsed}
Vector + ForwardEulerSolver + TimeDependentOperator + time/dt
                  -> ForwardEulerCheckpointAdapter
                  -> generic checkpoint core
\endcode

An application-defined specialization can use the same core without changing
it. For example, a future adaptive application could provide

\code{.unparsed}
Mesh + FESpace + fields + properties
                  -> application-defined adapter
                  -> generic checkpoint core
\endcode

This adaptive adapter is conceptual and is not implemented here.

\section checkpointing_components Core components

Snapshot is an owning opaque byte sequence with value semantics. Zero-length
snapshots are valid. MemoryCheckpointStorage and FileCheckpointStorage map a
logical CheckpointId to Snapshot bytes without interpreting application state.

ExactCheckpointWindow is a bounded FIFO cache of complete
`{StateId, Snapshot}` states. Capacity zero disables caching. Insertions are
staged so a failed copy does not commit a partial cache update.

CheckpointController borrows a CheckpointStateAdapter, StatePropagator,
CheckpointStorage, and ExactCheckpointWindow. Its active state means that the
external application is synchronized to a committed complete snapshot at the
reported StateId. CheckpointController::Restore() restores exactly the named
CheckpointId. CheckpointController::RestoreState() uses an exact target when
available, otherwise selects the nearest preceding cached or persisted state
and propagates forward. RestoreStep() is a compatibility spelling for
time-stepping applications.

StoreEverythingSchedule stores the initial state and every accepted logical
state. Scheduling is based on ordered state positions, never physical time.
More advanced schedules and reverse orchestration remain follow-up work.

Capture, restore, advance, and cache updates are staged around a committed
snapshot. On failure, the controller restores the prior complete snapshot. If
that rollback itself fails, the controller invalidates its active-state
metadata and CheckpointConsistencyError reports that the application and
controller can no longer be proven consistent. Capture is required to leave
the application unchanged even when it throws; Restore and Advance may expose
partial state only until controller rollback completes.

The generic Snapshot boundary adds one complete active-state snapshot. Advance
and restore transactions also make one rollback copy, and the moving window
retains its configured number of complete snapshot copies. This is the cost of
generic rollback without assuming an application-specific clone operation.

\section checkpointing_ode ODE and Vector specialization

ForwardEulerCheckpointAdapter binds externally owned Vector, TimePoint, `dt`,
ForwardEulerSolver, and TimeDependentOperator objects to
CheckpointStateAdapter. Its complete restart contains

\code{.unparsed}
Vector values + StateId + physical time + dt + solver restart bytes
\endcode

Forward Euler has no persistent stage history, so the solver restart bytes are
empty. Restore resets the Vector, TimePoint, and `dt`, sets the operator time,
and reinitializes the solver. ODEStatePropagator advances with the normal
ODESolver::Step() implementation. ODECheckpointPropagator and
ODESolverCheckpointAdapter remain compatibility names for the refined ODE
types.

Vector values are encoded by the ODE adapter using Vector::HostRead() and
restored using Vector::HostWrite(). Capture can therefore synchronize device
data to host. Persistent values use IEEE binary64; conversion from
single-precision real_t to binary64 and back preserves every original value.

\section checkpointing_persistence Persistence

The ODE adapter preserves the version 1 persistent byte format exactly. Its
64-byte canonical little-endian header is

\code{.unparsed}
offset  bytes  field
  0      8    magic 0x4d46454d43503100
  8      4    format version (1)
 12      1    byte-order marker (1 = little-endian)
 13      1    scalar width (8 = IEEE binary64)
 14      2    reserved, zero
 16      8    logical CheckpointId
 24      8    StateId (formerly StepId) bit representation
 32      8    physical time
 40      8    continuation dt
 48      8    Vector length
 56      8    solver restart payload length
 64     ...   Vector entries followed by solver restart bytes
\endcode

The meaning and byte positions are unchanged: StepId is now an alias for the
same signed 64-bit StateId, and the remaining fields are the ODE adapter's
opaque payload. Version 1 therefore remains valid. Generic application adapters
define and validate their own Snapshot encodings.

ODE decoding rejects unsupported versions, malformed lengths, ID or StateId
mismatches, truncation, and trailing bytes. Invalid bytes raise
InvalidCheckpointFormat; ordinary filesystem failures raise
CheckpointStorageError.

FileCheckpointStorage uses one deterministic file per logical checkpoint. An
overwrite is written and closed in a same-directory temporary file and then
renamed. A failed overwrite leaves the prior canonical file unchanged. Stale
temporary files are ignored. The implementation does not promise fsync-based
crash durability.

\section checkpointing_parallel Rank-local parallel compatibility

The core contains no MPI calls. For a distributed state
`S_i = {S_i^(0), ..., S_i^(P-1)}`, each rank's adapter captures its complete
local portion. Logical StateId and CheckpointId values remain common while
physical storage is rank-local, for example:

\code{.unparsed}
checkpoints/rank_000000/
checkpoints/rank_000001/
checkpoints/rank_000002/
\endcode

The application must coordinate errors around collective transitions. This
model does not provide collective commit, failure propagation, cross-rank
rollback, MPI-IO, a shared-file format, or fault-tolerant recovery.

\section checkpointing_ode_miniapp ODE miniapp

The `miniapps/checkpointing/ode-state.cpp` miniapp compares ordinary fixed-step
Forward Euler integration with a trajectory replayed from its initial exact
checkpoint:

\code{.sh}
cd miniapps/checkpointing
make checkpoint-ode-state
./checkpoint-ode-state
./checkpoint-ode-state -s 40
\endcode

It integrates `du/dt = alpha u - u^3`. StoreEverythingSchedule creates
checkpoint ID `step + 1` for every accepted state. The example then removes all
persistent checkpoints except the initial one, clears the transient window,
restores checkpoint 1, and reconstructs the terminal state. It reports

\code{.unparsed}
terminal replay error: 0
\endcode

and succeeds only when replay and ordinary integration are bitwise identical.

\section checkpointing_miniapps State-centric miniapps

Two serial miniapps demonstrate that the generic core is independent of Vector
and physical time. Both use an interval schedule that stores state zero and
selected nonterminal states. The terminal state is deliberately not stored.
The heterogeneous miniapp separately demonstrates persistent restore plus
replay and exact restoration from a moving window. Snapshot headers, lengths,
StateId values, and trailing bytes are validated during restore.

\subsection checkpointing_heterogeneous Heterogeneous application state

`miniapps/checkpointing/heterogeneous-state.cpp` owns this complete state:

\code{.cpp}
StateId iteration;
uint64_t fibonacci;
uint64_t next_fibonacci;
real_t floating_value;
std::string text;
\endcode

The two Fibonacci values are required to continue the recurrence. From state
zero, where `(fibonacci, next_fibonacci) = (0, 1)`, one transition applies

\f[
(F_k,F_{k+1}) \longmapsto (F_{k+1},F_k+F_{k+1}).
\f]

The floating-point recurrence is

\f[
x_0=1, \qquad x_{k+1}=0.5x_k+0.125.
\f]

The text begins as `state-0`; transition `k` appends `|state-k`. The adapter
encodes the iteration, both Fibonacci values, floating-point value, and complete
string in one opaque Snapshot. No Vector, ODESolver, TimeDependentOperator, or
physical time participates in this application state.

After the original run, the miniapp saves the terminal values only for
verification, overwrites the live state, restores the newest earlier interval
checkpoint, and calls CheckpointController::RestoreState() to replay to the
requested terminal StateId. Fibonacci and string values, including the hidden
next Fibonacci value, are compared exactly. The serialized floating-point value
and deterministic recurrence are also compared exactly.

To keep the recovery paths distinct, the miniapp clears the forward-run moving
window before persistent restore. Restore and replay repopulate it with the
terminal state. The application state is then overwritten a second time, and
RestoreState() recovers the exact non-persisted terminal snapshot from the
moving window. This cache is an optimization over complete snapshots; it does
not replace either Fibonacci continuation value.

Useful options are:

\code{.unparsed}
-n, --num-states N          number of sequence transitions (1-92)
-c, --checkpoint-interval C persist every C-th nonterminal state
-w, --window-size W         complete states retained in the moving window
\endcode

\subsection checkpointing_mesh Nonconforming Mesh state

`miniapps/checkpointing/mesh-state.cpp` starts from a programmatically generated
2-by-2 quadrilateral unit-square Mesh and calls Mesh::EnsureNCMesh(). StateId is
the number of completed refinement cycles. At each transition it recomputes the
valid target from the current mesh as

\code{.unparsed}
target = selection_index % mesh.GetNE()
\endcode

then refines that element and increments both `selection_index` and StateId. It
never retains an element reference across refinement.

The application adapter stores StateId, `selection_index`, and the result of
Mesh::Print(). Restore reconstructs the mesh with the MFEM stream constructor
and verifies that the payload is a nonempty two-dimensional nonconforming mesh.
Thus the application adapter, rather than the checkpoint core, defines and
restores the complete Mesh state.

After restoring an earlier interval checkpoint, the miniapp replays the
remaining refinement cycles and compares dimensions, element/vertex/boundary
counts, edge and face counts, attributes, element geometries, and exact
serialized mesh text. It then projects

\f[
c(x)=1+0.5x_0-0.25x_1
\f]

into order-`p` H1 spaces on both final meshes. Exact mesh replay gives identical
DOF layouts, so the fields are compared with the coefficient-vector infinity
norm using a tolerance of `100*epsilon`.

Useful options are:

\code{.unparsed}
-r, --refinement-steps N    number of refinement cycles
-c, --checkpoint-interval C persist every C-th nonterminal state
-p, --order P               H1 projection order
-o, --output-prefix PATH    parent ParaView output directory
-pv, --paraview             enable ParaView output (default)
-no-pv, --no-paraview       disable ParaView output
\endcode

When enabled, ParaViewDataCollection writes the mesh and the
`projected_coefficient` field to separate collections:

\code{.unparsed}
<output-prefix>/reference/
<output-prefix>/restored/
\endcode

The structural and projection comparisons determine success; ParaView output
is diagnostic only.

\subsection checkpointing_miniapps_running Building and testing

Build and run them with:

\code{.sh}
cd miniapps/checkpointing
make
./checkpoint-ode-state -s 40
./checkpoint-heterogeneous-state -n 12 -c 4 -w 2
./checkpoint-mesh-state -r 4 -c 2 -o paraview
\endcode

For automated smoke tests, enable `MFEM_ENABLE_TESTING` in a CMake build and
run:

\code{.sh}
ctest -R checkpoint- --output-on-failure
\endcode

The source directory also contains a makefile with corresponding sequential
test targets. See `miniapps/checkpointing/README.md` for a compact standalone
guide and expected default results.

\section checkpointing_compatibility Compatibility mapping

\code{.unparsed}
Previous concept                 State-centric concept
StepId                          StateId (StepId compatibility alias)
TimePoint                       ODE-specific helper
Vector CheckpointState          StateId + opaque Snapshot
ODESolverCheckpointAdapter      ODECheckpointStateAdapter compatibility name
ODECheckpointPropagator         ODEStatePropagator compatibility name
Vector-centric controller       generic CheckpointController
RestoreStep                     RestoreState (RestoreStep wrapper)
\endcode
*/
